INSERT INTO blog_post_translations
  (post_id, locale, slug, title, excerpt, body_html, seo_title, seo_description)
VALUES (
  :post_id, 'en', $x$nintendo-eshop-from-uzbekistan$x$,
  $x$Nintendo eShop from Uzbekistan: top up your Switch with your country's card$x$,
  $x$How to top up Nintendo eShop from Uzbekistan: which country your Nintendo Account is in, what happens to the balance if you change it, which eShop card to buy and how to redeem it.$x$,
$x$<p>You can top up Nintendo eShop from Uzbekistan with an eShop card from the country your Nintendo Account is registered in. Check the account's country first, then buy a card for that country and redeem the code on the console or on the web.</p>
<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>The eShop balance is tied to the Nintendo Account's country. Buy a card from the same country.</li>
<li>You can change the country on the Nintendo Account website, but your balance will not move with you.</li>
<li>In the Americas you must spend the balance to zero first. In Europe the change goes through, but the balance is lost after you confirm.</li>
<li>An active Nintendo Switch Online auto-renewal or a pre-ordered DLC blocks the change.</li>
<li>YuPay sells eShop cards for 10 countries; you pay in soʻm with Click, Payme or Uzum.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Which country your Nintendo Account is in</h2>
<p>Everything in eShop follows the account's country: the balance currency, prices, the catalogue, and which card will work. Players in Uzbekistan usually keep an account from another country — the USA, Poland, Germany, the UK — and top it up with that country's card.</p>
<p>Checking is simple. Sign in to the Nintendo Account website and open User Info. The country is shown in your profile. The console gives a hint too: the currency of your eShop balance. US dollars mean a US account, euros an account in a euro-area country, pounds the UK, złoty Poland.</p>
<p>If you already have an account with games on it, it is better to leave the country alone. Your purchases stay with the account, but the balance and subscriptions come with conditions when you switch.</p>
<h2>How to change the country and what happens to the balance</h2>
<p>The country is changed on the Nintendo Account website: User Info → Profile → Edit. Pick the new country and save.</p>
<p>The key issue is the money in your eShop balance. It does not move to the new country. The rule depends on where the account is now:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Account in the Americas</strong> (USA, Canada, Brazil and others). You can only change the country with a zero balance. Spend everything in eShop first.</li>
<li><strong>Account in Europe.</strong> You can change the country with money still in the balance, but it is gone once you confirm. Nintendo explicitly asks you to give up the balance — that is not a bug, it is a condition of the change.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you have money in the balance, spend it first. Do not top up the old region right before moving.</p>
<h2>What blocks a country change</h2>
<p>Even with a zero balance, the change will not go through while the account has:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nintendo Switch Online auto-renewal</strong> — turn it off in your Nintendo Account settings;</li>
<li><strong>pre-ordered DLC</strong> — add-ons paid for in advance that have not come out yet. Wait for the release or cancel the pre-order.</li>
</ul>
<p>Once these are cleared, change the country. From then on, top up with cards of the new country.</p>
<h2>Which eShop card to buy</h2>
<p>One rule: a card from the country your Nintendo Account is in. A card from another country may be refused when you redeem it, and its currency will not match your balance.</p>
<p>On YuPay, the <a href="/en/store/nintendo">Nintendo eShop</a> page has cards for 10 countries. The switcher at the top of the page picks the country:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=us">USA</a> — for a US account, in dollars;</li>
<li><a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=eu">Europe</a> — for an account in a euro-area country;</li>
<li><a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=de">Germany</a>, <a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=pl">Poland</a>, <a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=uk">United Kingdom</a>, <a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=se">Sweden</a>;</li>
<li><a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=ca">Canada</a>, <a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=br">Brazil</a>, <a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=jp">Japan</a>, <a href="/en/store/nintendo?region=hk">Hong Kong</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before paying, check two things: the card's country and your eShop balance currency. If they match, the code will work.</p>
<h2>How to buy a card on YuPay</h2>
<ol>
<li>Open <a href="/en/store/nintendo">Nintendo eShop</a> and pick your account's country.</li>
<li>Choose the amount.</li>
<li>Pay in soʻm with Click, Payme or Uzum. The total in soʻm is shown before payment, and that is exactly what is charged.</li>
<li>The code appears on the order page and is sent to your email.</li>
</ol>
<h2>How to redeem the code on Nintendo Switch and Switch 2</h2>
<ol>
<li>Open Nintendo eShop on the console.</li>
<li>Select the user linked to the right Nintendo Account.</li>
<li>In the left-hand menu, choose Redeem Code.</li>
<li>Enter the code from the card and confirm.</li>
</ol>
<p>The amount is added to your eShop balance. You can spend it from the console or on the Nintendo website.</p>
<p>You can also redeem without the console: the Nintendo website has a code redemption page. Sign in to the same Nintendo Account and enter the code there. Handy when the console is not at hand.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A card from the wrong country.</strong> A US card on a European account is the most common reason for a refusal. Account country first, card second.</li>
<li><strong>Topping up before a country change.</strong> The money stays in the old region: in the Americas the change will not go through, in Europe the balance is lost.</li>
<li><strong>The wrong user on the console.</strong> If your Switch has several profiles, check which Nintendo Account the selected one is linked to.</li>
<li><strong>A typo in the code.</strong> Copy the code from the order page instead of retyping it. Similar-looking characters get mixed up.</li>
<li><strong>A forgotten auto-renewal.</strong> The country change fails, and the cause is the Nintendo Switch Online subscription. Turn auto-renewal off in advance.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Other platforms</h2>
<p>PlayStation and Xbox have their own country rules. On PlayStation the account's country cannot be changed at all; on Microsoft it can, but not more than once every three months. Details are in our guides on <a href="/en/blog/how-to-buy-ps-store-games-from-uzbekistan">how to buy PS Store games from Uzbekistan</a> and <a href="/en/blog/how-to-top-up-xbox-from-uzbekistan">how to top up Xbox from Uzbekistan</a>.</p>
<p>Checked your account's country? Open <a href="/en/store/nintendo">Nintendo eShop on YuPay</a>, pick the same country and the amount you need.</p>$x$,
  $x$Nintendo eShop from Uzbekistan — how to top up your Switch$x$,
  $x$How to top up a Nintendo Switch from Uzbekistan: Nintendo Account country, changing region and the eShop balance, the right card and redeeming the code.$x$
);

